        <article class="article-card">
          <h2 class="card-title">
            <a href="/articles/tales-of-two-comets-a1-maps-and-r3-pan-starrs-both-make-a-showing-in-april">Tales of Two Comets: A1 MAPS and R3 Pan-STARRS Both Make a Showing in April</a>
          </h2>

          <div class="card-meta">
            <span>
              By <a href="/authors/david-dickinson.html">David Dickinson</a> - March 31, 2026 05:12 PM UTC | <a href="/categories/observing.html">Observing</a>
            </span>
          </div>

          <a href="/articles/tales-of-two-comets-a1-maps-and-r3-pan-starrs-both-make-a-showing-in-april">
            
              <img src="/article_images/Michael-Jaeger-Gerald-Rhemann-2025R320260323UT330sec9x160web_1774273219_lg_20260331_162833.jpg" alt="" class="featured-image">
            
          </a>

          <div class="card-excerpt">
            
              All eyes are on the inner solar system in April 2026, as two comets reach perihelion. One, Comet R3 Pan-STARRS we’ve known about since last year. Another, sungrazer A1 MAPS was just found as the first comet of 2026 and presents us with a big question: will it survive its blistering perihelion passage on Saturday, April 4th, or simply vaporize like the majority of sungrazers before it?
            
          </div>

          <a href="/articles/tales-of-two-comets-a1-maps-and-r3-pan-starrs-both-make-a-showing-in-april" class="read-more">Continue reading</a>
